1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,923
Every organisation runs cryptography it cannot fully see.

2
00:00:04,923 --> 00:00:07,849
A call inside a payments service.

3
00:00:07,849 --> 00:00:10,917
A version pin in a dependency file.

4
00:00:10,917 --> 00:00:14,408
A cipher list nobody has opened in years.

5
00:00:14,408 --> 00:00:17,900
The signature algorithm on a certificate.

6
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:23,230
A private key baked into a container image that shipped months ago.

7
00:00:23,230 --> 00:00:28,771
Six kinds of artefact — and no two describe cryptography the same way.

8
00:00:29,521 --> 00:00:32,530
CryptoDrishti reads all of it,

9
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:36,871
and turns it into one inventory you can defend.

10
00:00:37,621 --> 00:00:41,011
A real scan of the demonstration estate.

11
00:00:41,011 --> 00:00:45,031
23 distinct assets, 16 of them quantum-vulnerable

12
00:00:45,031 --> 00:00:49,751
— and the tool says first that the inventory is incomplete,

13
00:00:49,751 --> 00:00:52,650
because one endpoint was refused.

14
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:59,202
Every asset gets a row: a score, what a quantum computer does to it,

15
00:00:59,202 --> 00:01:03,410
where the evidence was found, how strong it is,

16
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:06,250
and the replacement it needs.

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,997
That one scan found five RSA findings.

18
00:01:10,997 --> 00:01:15,598
The first is a signing call — RSA-PSS padding

19
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:18,820
— so the target is ML-DSA-65.

20
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:25,143
The second is RSA-OAEP wrapping a key: that is key establishment,

21
00:01:25,143 --> 00:01:29,313
and the target is a hybrid key exchange.

22
00:01:29,313 --> 00:01:32,879
A completely different migration.

23
00:01:32,879 --> 00:01:36,963
The third is a cipher list that permits

24
00:01:36,963 --> 00:01:40,443
RSA without ever saying what for

25
00:01:40,443 --> 00:01:44,354
— so the tool names no target at all.

26
00:01:44,354 --> 00:01:50,850
Knowing the algorithm is not enough. Purpose decides the migration.

27
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:55,096
Seven workstreams over 21 call sites

28
00:01:55,096 --> 00:01:59,930
— one per replacement algorithm, not one per finding.

29
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:04,450
That is the difference between a list and a plan.

30
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:09,640
Open any finding and the chain is there: the call site,

31
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:12,609
the symbol matched, the technique,

32
00:02:12,609 --> 00:02:17,048
how strong the evidence is that the key is really used,

33
00:02:17,048 --> 00:02:20,157
and the arithmetic behind its score.

34
00:02:20,907 --> 00:02:26,267
It is just as careful about what it did not see.

35
00:02:26,267 --> 00:02:31,627
A key written in one container layer and deleted

36
00:02:31,627 --> 00:02:35,942
in the next is reported as historical

37
00:02:35,942 --> 00:02:40,257
— gone at runtime, still extractable.

38
00:02:40,257 --> 00:02:46,757
And a refused endpoint makes the scan PARTIAL, and says why.

39
00:02:47,507 --> 00:02:52,494
The same scan also comes out as a document: exposure,

40
00:02:52,494 --> 00:02:56,994
the migration programme grouped by replacement,

41
00:02:56,994 --> 00:03:00,357
and a stated limitations section.

42
00:03:01,107 --> 00:03:10,431
And for the tooling: a CycloneDX cryptographic bill of materials

43
00:03:10,431 --> 00:03:19,883
— the published standard — validated offline against the official

44
00:03:19,883 --> 00:03:24,957
JSON Schema at a pinned commit.

45
00:03:25,707 --> 00:03:30,161
None of this has to be taken on trust

46
00:03:30,161 --> 00:03:37,557
— every figure in this film comes from a command in the repository.

47
00:03:38,307 --> 00:03:43,087
Scattered evidence becomes one explainable inventory,

48
00:03:43,087 --> 00:03:46,621
a risk assessment you can argue with,

49
00:03:46,621 --> 00:03:50,389
and a migration decision you can defend.

50
00:03:50,389 --> 00:03:54,157
CryptoDrishti · Team Zero-Day · SIH26164
